<h1>Forms and Certificates</h1>

<p>
    Quickfire includes powerful PDF editing tools that let you work with forms and certificates directly in the system. Unlike simple file attachments, these are "live" PDFs that you can edit, version, and manage without leaving Quickfire.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of the PDF form editor showing a form being edited with fields filled in. Show the version history panel on the side. Include labels for key areas: editor, version list, save button, etc. -->

<h2>Live PDF Forms</h2>

<p>
    Forms in Quickfire are interactive PDFs that you can edit directly in your browser. This is different from just viewing or downloading a PDF—you can actually type into form fields, make changes, and save new versions.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>In-app editing</strong> – Type directly into PDF form fields without downloading or using external software</li>
    <li><strong>Version history</strong> – Every save creates a new version, so you never lose previous work</li>
    <li><strong>Non-destructive</strong> – Saving never overwrites—it always creates a new revision</li>
    <li><strong>Revert capability</strong> – If someone makes a mistake, you can go back to a previous version</li>
    <li><strong>Full history</strong> – See who made what changes and when</li>
</ul>

<p>
    This makes forms perfect for ACORD forms, applications, and other documents that need to be filled out and might need changes later.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Split-screen view: Left side shows the PDF editor with a form being filled in, right side shows the version history list. Use callouts to highlight: editing fields, version list, save button, revert option. -->

<h2>How Version History Works</h2>

<p>
    Every time you save a form, Quickfire creates a new version instead of overwriting the old one. This means:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>All versions are preserved</strong> – You can see every version that's ever been saved</li>
    <li><strong>Easy comparison</strong> – View different versions to see what changed</li>
    <li><strong>Revert when needed</strong> – If someone edits the wrong field, reopen a previous version and continue from there</li>
    <li><strong>Complete audit trail</strong> – See who made changes and when</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>To revert to a previous version:</strong>
</p>

<ol>
    <li>Open the form</li>
    <li>View the version history (usually in a sidebar or panel)</li>
    <li>Find the version you want to go back to</li>
    <li>Click to reopen that version</li>
    <li>Continue working from that point</li>
</ol>

<p>
    The old versions aren't deleted—they're all still available, so you can switch between them as needed.
</p>

