<h2>Working with Form Fields</h2>

<p>
    When editing a form, you'll see different types of fields:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Text fields</strong> – Type directly into these</li>
    <li><strong>Checkboxes</strong> – Click to check or uncheck</li>
    <li><strong>Dropdowns</strong> – Select from a list of options</li>
    <li><strong>Date fields</strong> – Use a date picker or type dates</li>
</ul>

<p>
    The form editor works just like filling out a PDF in Adobe Reader or another PDF viewer, but everything happens in Quickfire and is automatically saved with version history.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Close-up of a form showing different field types: text field being typed into, checkbox being checked, dropdown being opened. Use visual indicators to show the interaction. -->

<h2>Attaching Forms and Certificates</h2>

<p>
    Once you've created or edited a form or certificate, you can attach it to:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Clients</strong> – Store it with the client record</li>
    <li><strong>Renewals</strong> – Attach it to a renewal</li>
    <li><strong>Policies</strong> – Link it to a policy</li>
    <li><strong>Proposals</strong> – Include it in a proposal</li>
</ul>

<p>
    When you attach a form, the version history is preserved, so you can always see what was sent and revert if needed.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot showing the attachment options or process. Show a form being attached to a client record or renewal. Include the version information in the attachment. -->

<h2>Downloading and Sharing</h2>

<p>
    You can download forms and certificates as PDFs to:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Email them directly to clients or carriers</li>
    <li>Print them</li>
    <li>Store them locally</li>
    <li>Share them outside Quickfire</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Downloaded PDFs are "flattened" (form fields are converted to regular text), so they can be viewed and printed in any PDF viewer without needing the original form.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot showing the download option or button. Show a downloaded PDF being opened in a PDF viewer. Include a note about flattening. -->

<h2>Best Practices</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Use policy auto-fill</strong> – Always use the policy dropdown for certificates to save time and reduce errors</li>
    <li><strong>Save regularly</strong> – Don't lose work—save frequently, especially for long forms</li>
    <li><strong>Review before saving</strong> – Check that all fields are correct before saving</li>
    <li><strong>Use version history</strong> – If something looks wrong, check previous versions</li>
    <li><strong>Revert when needed</strong> – Don't be afraid to go back to a previous version if mistakes were made</li>
    <li><strong>Attach to records</strong> – Link forms to clients or renewals so they're easy to find</li>
</ul>

